[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"String Matching and Manipulation Algorithms in Problem Solving"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"String manipulation and pattern matching are fundamental topics in computer science and algorithmic problem solving. Efficiently finding patterns within large texts or transforming strings is crucial in domains ranging from bioinformatics to search engines and compiler design."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Naive String Matching Algorithm"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The most straightforward approach to finding a pattern of length m in a text of length n is the naive algorithm. It checks for a match at all possible positions i in the text, leading to a worst-case time complexity of O(n * m)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"def naive_search(text, pattern):
    n = len(text)
    m = len(pattern)
    results = []
    for i in range(n - m + 1):
        match = True
        for j in range(m):
            if text[i + j] != pattern[j]:
                match = False
                break
        if match:
            results.append(i)
    return results"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Advanced String Matching: The Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP) Algorithm"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To improve upon the naive approach, the KMP algorithm utilizes preprocessing to avoid unnecessary comparisons. By constructing a Longest Prefix Suffix (LPS) array, KMP shifts the pattern efficiently when a mismatch occurs, reducing the time complexity to O(n + m)."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"def compute_lps(pattern, m, lps):
    length = 0
    i = 1
    lps[0] = 0
    while i < m:
        if pattern[i] == pattern[length]:
            length += 1
            lps[i] = length
            i += 1
        else:
            if length != 0:
                length = lps[length - 1]
            else:
                lps[i] = 0
                i += 1

def kmp_search(text, pattern):
    n = len(text)
    m = len(pattern)
    lps = [0] * m
    results = []
    compute_lps(pattern, m, lps)
    i = 0
    j = 0
    while i < n:
        if pattern[j] == text[i]:
            i += 1
            j += 1
        if j == m:
            results.append(i - j)
            j = lps[j - 1]
        elif i < n and pattern[j] != text[i]:
            if j != 0:
                j = lps[j - 1]
            else:
                i += 1
    return results"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Common String Manipulation Techniques"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Two-Pointer Technique: Useful for checking palindromes or reversing strings in-place with O(1) extra space."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sliding Window: Ideal for substring problems, such as finding the longest substring without repeating characters."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hashing (Rabin-Karp): Uses rolling hashes to find patterns in expected O(n + m) time."},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering these algorithms equips you to tackle complex coding interview questions and performance-critical production systems effectively."}]
